    structure: "Every collection point (form, API, SDK, log line, telemetry event) is tied to a documented purpose, and each field is justified against that purpose or removed. The structure has four parts. A purpose register lists what each system collects and why, owned by the product team, reviewed by privacy. A collection gate at design time (a short review in the design document template) asks for each new field whether the purpose needs it, whether a coarser form would do (age band instead of birth date, region instead of address, hashed identifier instead of name) and when it will be deleted. At runtime, collection code drops or coarsens fields before storage: truncating IP addresses, bucketing timestamps, redacting free text, rejecting unexpected fields at the API boundary. Downstream, a data map shows which stores hold which fields so that any field can be traced to its purpose, and a periodic query finds fields that have not been read in a set period, which are candidates for removal. The structure is enforced by default settings: new systems collect the minimum until a purpose justifies more.",
