    tier: "emerging",
    statement: "Adding calibrated noise so that any published statistic is nearly the same whether or not one person's record is included defeats re-identification attacks that no anonymisation by deletion has survived.",
    origin: "Cynthia Dwork, Frank McSherry, Kobbi Nissim and Adam Smith defined differential privacy in 2006, giving a mathematical bound (epsilon) on how much any one individual's presence can change the output of an analysis. Google deployed local differential privacy in Chrome through RAPPOR in 2014 and Apple adopted it for device telemetry in 2016. The US Census Bureau adopted it for the 2020 Census after an internal reconstruction attack on 2010 data re-identified a substantial share of the population from published tables, showing that traditional suppression and swapping no longer protected respondents.",
    structure: "The structure has a trusted boundary, a mechanism and a budget. In the central model, raw records sit inside a trusted boundary and only noisy answers leave it: each query's sensitivity (how much one record can change the answer) is computed, noise scaled to that sensitivity and to epsilon is added, and the result is released. In the local model, each device adds noise before sending, so the collector never sees a true individual value; this needs more data for the same accuracy but removes the trusted collector. A privacy budget (total epsilon over a period) is allocated to the dataset and spent by each release; an accountant tracks spend and refuses queries once the budget is exhausted, because privacy loss composes across releases. Mechanisms differ by task: Laplace or Gaussian noise for counts and sums, exponential mechanism for selection, specialised algorithms for histograms and synthetic data. The released epsilon and the mechanism are published so that users of the data can reason about accuracy and privacy. Accuracy is checked against the use case before release, since small populations and rare categories lose the most.",
