    statement: "A designed line between what may be written to a medium that cannot be erased (public ledgers, transparency logs, locked backups, trained models) and what must stay erasable defeats the irreversible disclosure that no later control can undo.",
    origin: "The boundary emerged from three collisions. Public blockchains made writing permanent and global, and the EDPB's 2025 guidelines on blockchain processing of personal data state that on chain personal data cannot be erased, so the right to erasure must be met by design, typically by keeping personal data off chain and deleting the off chain keys or data that link to it. Transparency logs and write once backups created the same problem for operational data. Machine learning models that memorise training data created it for inference, and regulators responded with algorithmic disgorgement orders (the US Federal Trade Commission required Everalbum in 2021 and Weight Watchers' Kurbo in 2022 to delete models trained on unlawfully collected data). The common structure is a decision made before the write, because after it there is no decision left.",
    structure: "The organisation enumerates its permanent media: public and consortium ledgers, transparency and audit logs with append only semantics, backups and archives under retention locks, trained model weights, published datasets and anything copied to a party outside its control. For each medium, a rule states what may be written: by default only data that is non personal and non secret, or data that has passed through an irreversibility control. The irreversibility controls are a short list: cryptographic commitment (a hash or salted commitment on chain, the data itself off chain and erasable), encryption before write with keys held in an erasable store so that key destruction is erasure (cryptographic erase under NIST SP 800-88), pseudonymisation with the mapping held off medium, differential privacy for aggregates and model training, and consent plus documented purpose for the rare case where permanence is itself the purpose. A gate before each permanent write checks the rule: a code review requirement for anything that writes to chain, a schema check on what enters an append only log, a training data review before a model is trained. A register records what is on each permanent medium and under which control, so an erasure request can be answered by destroying the key or mapping rather than by failing. The boundary is reviewed whenever a new permanent medium is adopted.",
